## 2. Contract proposal and product behavior

All of this is proposed unless §10 marks it ruled.

### 2.1 Near-misses (NM1, NM3)

**Where.** On every blocker of kind `atom` (positive body atom), at any descent
depth. Not on `negated`, `comparison` or `head` blockers (NM-D6).

**Normalization `N(v)`** (NM-D4, `(o1)`), defined only for `str`:

1. Unicode NFKC;
2. `str.casefold()`;
3. delete every character whose Unicode general category starts with `P`;
4. replace each maximal run of characters for which `str.isspace()` is true
   with one U+0020, then strip leading and trailing U+0020.

`N(v)` is undefined for non-`str` values (including `bool`), and a value with
`N(v) == ""` never near-matches. Consequences stated in the docs: `ACME-CORP`
near-matches `ACMECORP`, not `ACME CORP`; `Acme . Corp` near-matches
`Acme Corp`. (p1-P) `N` follows the running interpreter's Unicode database.
(p1-Q)

**Eligible rows** (p1-A). Let the blocker's atom have roles `R`, partitioned
into constant roles, bound-variable roles `B` (variables bound in the bindings
that reached the blocker) and free-variable roles. A row `f` of the atom's fact
type (the rows the join read, `_Walk.facts`) is **eligible** when it passes
every per-row check production applies in `_joined_atom_candidates` except the
comparison of bound roles with the binding: every constant role matches by
identity, and every free variable that occurs in two or more roles has the same
identity at each of them. A bound variable occurring in two or more roles is
compared at each of them against the binding (exactly or by `N`).

**Near-miss pair (row-level, NM-D3, `(o1)`).** For a binding `b` that reached
the blocker and an eligible row `f`, `(b, f)` is a near-miss when every role in
`B` matches exactly or `N` is defined and equal on both sides, and at least one
role in `B` differs exactly. A blocker with `B` empty has no near-misses.

**Counting without enumerating** (p1-C). Key each eligible row and each binding
by the tuple, over sorted `B`, of `("n", N(v))` when defined and else
`("x", identity(v))`. Within one key, every (distinct `B`-projection of a
binding, distinct `B`-projection of an eligible row) combination is a
near-miss, because an exact match would have joined (the exactness invariant).
`near_miss_count` is the sum over keys of the two distinct-projection counts'
product. The reported pairs are the 5 smallest by
`(canonical(sample), canonical(candidate))`, built from at most 5 sorted samples
× 5 sorted candidates per key. Cost: one inspection per eligible row and per
binding.

**Truncation** (p1-C). The search has its own inspection counter, capped by a
new `WhyNotLimits.near_miss_inspections` (default 200,000, checked like the
other ceilings). Reaching the cap stops the search and sets
`near_miss_truncated: true`; `near_miss_count` is then a lower bound and the
pairs are those found so far. The counter is not part of `work`. The request's
status and exit are unaffected.

**Payload (raw view).**

```json
"near_miss_count": 1,
"near_miss_truncated": false,
"near_misses": [
  {"roles": ["party"],
   "sample": {"party": "Acme Corp.", "case": "c-9"},
   "candidate": {"party": "ACME CORP.", "case": "c-9"}}
]
```

`sample` is `b` projected onto `B`, keyed by role; `candidate` is `f` projected
onto `B`; `roles` is the sorted set of roles differing exactly. The three fields
are present on every atom blocker once the search is enabled; an empty list with
count 0 means "searched, none". Redacted view: `near_miss_count` and
`near_miss_truncated` are shown; `near_misses` is `"redacted"`.

**Enablement** (p1-B). The kernel takes `near_misses: bool = False`. In NM1 no
public path enables it, so NM1 changes no public byte. NM3 enables it in the
service together with the schema bump.

### 2.2 Disclosure mode (NM2)

**Values (NM-D1 ruled `(o1)`; shape NM-D12 ruled `(p1-E)`).**

| Mode | Check family (declared redaction) | Witness lane (`witness`, `witness-view`, `replay`, `why-not`) and exports (`lean`, `souffle*`) | Raw requests |
|---|---|---|---|
| unset | today | today | today |
| `internal` | not applied (as `unredacted=True`) | raw in CLI and API | allowed |
| `customer` | applied | redacted in CLI and API: `api.why_not` returns the redacted view; `api.witness` and `api.replay` return the CLI's default view; no raw certificate | refused |

`customer` therefore withholds declared-sensitive values in check-family
outputs and every population value in witness-lane and export outputs. It does
not redact undeclared values in check-family messages; the docs say so and
point to `redact: true` declarations. (p1-E)

**Unchanged in every mode** (NM-D13, `(p1-G)`). The three session output kinds
(agent feedback, attempts, results: their wire fixes `applied`, and feedback
goes to agents that may be external providers), `discover` (2026-10-25 ruling)
and ILP export (keeps refusing declared roles). In `customer`, an explicit raw
request on these is still refused.

**Setting it (NM-D2, proposed).** Environment variable `GND_DISCLOSURE`, value
exactly `internal` or `customer`; and a global CLI option
`gnd --disclosure {internal,customer}`. Precedence (p1-D):

- If the environment says `customer`, the option may only say `customer`;
  `--disclosure internal` is refused.
- Otherwise the option, if given, wins for that invocation; else the
  environment; else unset.

No per-call API keyword. The CLI sets a context variable in `gnd.disclosure`;
API functions read it, else the environment, so the CLI's precedence survives
its calls into `gnd.api`.

**Refusals and bands** (p1-K). A bad or repeated `--disclosure` value is a usage
error, exit 64, through the existing `_BandSafeParser`/`_StoreOnce` path. A bad
`GND_DISCLOSURE` value (anything else, including `Customer` or the empty string)
is refused only by commands that have a disclosure site, with exit 3 and a
message naming the variable, never its value; other commands ignore it. A raw
request in `customer` and a loosening option exit 3. The exact refusal payload
per lane (check-family envelope, why-not and witness envelopes, export, judgment
error reason, API `ValueError` text) is specified in the NM2 spec; any enum
addition is recorded there as a schema change.

**Child processes** (p1-L). `gnd solve` forwards the resolved mode to the
stations it starts. `exp run` adds `GND_DISCLOSURE` (the resolved value) to the
agent environment allowlist, so an agent that runs `gnd` inherits the lock.

**Reporting.** Outputs that report a redaction mode keep their field; why-not
keeps `view`. No new field in any check-family report.

## 3. Dependencies and artifact homes

NM1 and NM2 are independent. NM3 needs both. NM4 needs NM3; its checkpoint DC1
needs only the approved plan, DC1b needs NM2. Close follows NM4. Nothing depends
on provider review, downstream execution, publication or certification.

| Artifact | Home |
|---|---|
| Plan, specs, records, reviews | `.gnd-planning/milestones/why-not-near-misses/` |
| Normalization and near-miss search | `src/gnd/witness/why_not.py` (NM1) |
| Disclosure resolver | `src/gnd/disclosure.py`, layer-map entry beside `gnd.redaction` (NM2; NM-D7) |
| CLI option | root parser in `src/gnd/cli.py` (NM2) |
| Packaged example with a `redact: true` role | `src/gnd/examples/disclosure/` (name fixed in the NM2 spec), listed in `tools/check_installed_docs.py` (NM2) (p1-O) |
| Example population with a spelling gap | `src/gnd/examples/quoted_claims/spelling-gap.pop.json` (NM3) |
| Schema | `src/gnd/why-not.schema.json`, `gnd-why-not/2` (NM3) |
| Goldens | NM1: inline kernel fixtures in `tests/test_why_not_kernel.py`; NM3: `tests/fixtures/why_not/` regenerated (p1-P) |
| Docs | NM3: normative near-miss section and how-to section; NM4: everything else (p1-N) |

## 4. Phases

### NM1: Near-miss search in the kernel (about 110 source lines, 350 test lines)

Spec: [phase-NM1-spec.md](phase-NM1-spec.md), r2, blocked on plan approval.

- **NM1-C1:** `N` as §2.1, one function; unit cases discriminating step order
  and deletion versus replacement.
- **NM1-C2:** eligible rows, keyed counting, the 5 reported pairs, at every
  descent depth, behind `near_misses=False`; the exactness invariant raised as
  `WhyNotInternalError`.
- **NM1-C3:** the independent oracle gains its own brute-force near-miss
  computation (own normalization, own eligibility); its random generator gains
  spelling variants; agreement on every case, with a minimum number of
  non-empty and truncated reports asserted.
- **NM1-C4:** the inspection counter and cap with literal truncation tests,
  including the n×n case that was quadratic in r1.
- **NM1-C5:** with the flag off, every existing kernel, oracle and public test
  and golden is unchanged.

### NM2: Disclosure mode (about 300 source lines, 500 test lines; about 14 modules)

- **NM2-C1:** `gnd.disclosure` resolver and context variable; precedence and
  tighten-only rule; refusals and bands as §2.2; hostile values (empty, very
  long, non-ASCII, mixed case) cased.
- **NM2-C2:** the site table, built mechanically from (i) a walk of the root
  parser tree for `--raw` and `--unredacted` actions, (ii) `inspect.signature`
  over `gnd.api.__all__`, its classes' public methods and `gnd.export.service`
  for `raw` and `unredacted` parameters, and (iii) `redaction.emitter_specs()`.
  Each discovered site has a row stating its behaviour per mode; a site without
  a row fails the test. Known rows at `33c260e`: 4 witness-lane views,
  `witness --out`, 4 export raw parsers and their 4 Python functions,
  `judgments --unredacted` and `api.judgments`, `api.check_with_receipt`,
  `EvaluationSession.check/check_with_receipt/transition`, `api.witness`,
  `api.replay`, the 13 output kinds, ILP export and `discover`. (p1-F)
- **NM2-C3:** a `customer` leak test per site using the packaged redaction
  example and why-not values: declared-sensitive values absent from check-family
  outputs; all population values absent from witness-lane and export outputs;
  every raw request and loosening option refused with the specified payload.
  `internal`: raw per site. "Unchanged in every mode" rows: identical bytes
  across modes.
- **NM2-C4:** child processes: one test for `solve` forwarding and one for the
  `exp run` agent environment.
- **NM2-C5:** unset parity on every existing contract fixture; regenerated RF1
  goldens listed (12 cli-exit goldens with the root usage line, `cli/tree.json`,
  three `cli-help/gnd.py31{1,3,4}.json`). (p1-J)
- **NM2-C6:** environment isolation: an autouse fixture in `tests/conftest.py`
  and `run_pages` in `tools/run_tutorials.py` remove `GND_DISCLOSURE`; a negative
  control sets it ambiently and shows the parity gates unchanged. (p1-M)
- **NM2-C7:** the packaged redaction example; CLI help for `--disclosure`.

### NM3: Public surface (about 60 source lines, 250 test lines)

Approval instrument: corpus-first (goldens for both views, schema negatives,
exits), as WN2.

- **NM3-C1:** the service enables the search; `gnd why-not` and
  `gnd.api.why_not` emit the three fields in both views and honour the mode.
- **NM3-C2:** schema `gnd-why-not/2`: the three fields in `$defs/blocker`;
  `near_misses: {"const": "redacted"}` in `redacted_clause.blocker`; negative
  cases (raw `near_misses` under `view: redacted`, missing field, count below the
  list length). Regenerated: `tests/fixtures/why_not/goldens`,
  `tests/fixtures/rf5-handlers/answers.json` (`gnd-why-not/1` → `/2`), and the
  docs outputs that print whole payloads. (p1-H, p1-P)
- **NM3-C3:** the spelling-gap example population and a committed repair test
  built only from payload fields, with a negative control (a candidate whose
  other bound role differs is not reported).
- **NM3-C4:** docs owned here (p1-N): the normative near-miss section of
  `docs/reference/why-not.md` (the one home of `N` and the rule, moving from the
  NM1 spec); the section "When the values are spelled differently" in
  `docs/how-to/repair-a-missing-claim.md`; the regenerated why-not outputs in
  that page and `python-api.md`; a release-note entry with the `/2` migration
  line. Docs gates exit 0.

### NM4: Documentation

House style per `docs/development/documentation.md`; anonymity per NM-R8. No
business tutorial page is in scope; if the owner adds one, it shows skill
prompts and results, not code. Every output shown comes from a real run
(`gnd-run` blocks executed by `tools/run_tutorials.py`); nothing is fabricated.

**Checkpoints (owner-visible, before the work is complete).** (p1-O)

- **DC1, after plan approval:** drafts of `reference/disclosure.md` and the
  near-miss section of `reference/why-not.md`, rendered locally, with example
  payloads marked *illustrative*. The owner's reaction can amend NM2/NM3 specs.
  Not committed to `docs/`.
